# ============================================================
# LIFE.TXT  -  one big text file (OBTF): journal, ideas, notes, and todos,
# in one chronological stream.
#
# Structure comes from :tags:, not separate files.
#
# Entry format:   YYYY-MM-DD Day | free text here :tag: :tag:
#                 boxed between two 80-dash rules.
# Separator:      a *** line has double purpose: it marks the START of
#                 an entry, OR, as the file's last line with nothing
#                 after it, marks end-of-file instead (visual only;
#                 agenda ignores it either way).
#
# Tag style: LIFE.TXT uses colon-delimited :tag: tokens, space-separated
# (one token each: :todo: :work:), NOT chained (:todo:work:).
# 
# The colons delimit tags within free-form prose, where @ alone could be 
# ambiguous against ordinary words.
# 
# calendar.txt uses the same :tag: style; there @ is
# reserved for time (@10am, @1pm-3pm), so the two files share one tag syntax.
# 
# How agenda reads this file:
#   open todo       = a line with :todo:    and NOT :done:/:dropped:
#   open waiting    = a line with :waiting: and NOT :done:/:dropped:
#   closed item     = add :done: in place; it then drops out of agenda
#   dropped item    = add :dropped: in place; same effect as :done:
#   due follow-up   = add  due:YYYY-MM-DD  to a line; shows in DUE only
#                      (not also in TODO/WAITING) once inside its lead
#                      window or overdue (see: agenda --help)
#   completed date  = add  completed:YYYY-MM-DD  to a line; excludes it
#                      from every section on its own -- no :done: tag
#                      required, the date itself is enough
#   threshold date  = add  threshold:YYYY-MM-DD  to a line; agenda hides
#                      it everywhere until that date arrives
#
# NOTE: This is a template.
# Example dates are illustrative - replace with your own.
# Location is adjustable: agenda reads it from $LIFE_DIR
# (default ~/life, so ~/life/LIFE.TXT); set LIFE_DIR to point elsewhere,
# e.g. /mnt/usbflashdrive/life/LIFE.TXT on a machine using a USB drive.
# ============================================================

:tag: CONVENTIONS
---------------------
Tags are keywords enclosed in colons, opened AND closed: :like_this:
Lowercase, underscores for spaces.
Every tag opens and closes with a colon.
Tags are space-separated, one token each: :todo: :work: -- NOT chained
  org-mode style (:todo:work:).
  Deliberate + org-independent: adopting org later means a full outline 
  conversion, not a colon-syntax match; a clean space-separated file converts
  more easily, not less.
  Decided; don't reopen.

State:  :todo: :waiting: :done: :someday: :dropped:
        (grep ':todo:' LIFE.TXT  finds all open actions; swap state as needed)
Area:   :work: :health: :career: :family:
        (grep ':health:' LIFE.TXT  finds all entries in that area)
Kind:   :reference:   non-actionable lookup data (measurements, specs, etc.)
        (grep ':reference:' LIFE.TXT  lists all; agenda ignores these by design)
Project:  :project_NAME:
          (grep -o ':project_[a-z0-9_]*:' LIFE.TXT | sort -u  lists all)
Dated:  :todo: with  due:YYYY-MM-DD   (due: is a key:value token, NOT a tag --
        no surrounding colons, always its own space-separated token, same
        shape as completed:/threshold: below)
        (grep 'due:' LIFE.TXT  finds all; grep 'due:2026-' for a year)
Completed:  :done: with  completed:YYYY-MM-DD   (records when an item was
        closed, and excludes the line from every section on its own --
        no :done: tag required, the date itself is enough.
        Named "completed:", not "done:", so it can't be confused with the
        :done: state tag.)
        (grep 'completed:' LIFE.TXT  finds all)
Threshold:  threshold:YYYY-MM-DD   (hides an entry from TODO/WAITING/DUE
        entirely until that date arrives, then it behaves normally -- for
        "don't show me this yet", not "remind me by".
        Works even combined with due:; a future threshold suppresses the
        whole entry.)
        (grep 'threshold:' LIFE.TXT  finds all)
No contexts (@home/@errands): tasks aren't location-gated here.
Area covers grouping; reintroduce :context_NAME: only if real
location-batching ever appears.

ENTRY STRUCTURE
*** now has DOUBLE PURPOSE (revised 2026-11-07):
  1. Marks the START of an entry. It is a LEADING marker, not a wrapping
     pair -- one *** opens the entry that follows it. The same line
     visually reads as "closing" the entry above it too, but structurally
     it's one event shared by both.
  2. As the LAST line of the file, with nothing after it, marks
     end-of-file instead. A trailing *** does NOT start an empty entry --
     don't add a header box after it. This is the only context where ***
     means something other than purpose 1.
  Both purposes use the identical three-asterisk-cluster line; nothing in
  the character sequence itself distinguishes them -- position in the file
  (last line vs. not) is what tells them apart. Decided; don't reopen.
  ***           = entry start (purpose 1), or end-of-file if it's the
                   file's last line (purpose 2) -- never appears inside
                   an entry's body
  blank line    = paragraph break WITHIN an entry's body (one idea per
                   paragraph); also the single blank line between an
                   entry's last line and the next ***  (see spacing below)
  plain newline = semantic line break between sentences of one paragraph
The *** marker is three asterisk-clusters on their own line; exact spacing
  and column alignment are NOT significant -- recognizability is the whole
  job, so don't fuss over aligning them or normalizing old ones.
  Canonical form is bare *** (three characters, nothing else on the line);
  use that for all new entries. A wider, spaced-out form may still appear
  on old lines -- still valid under the rule above, just not the form to
  write going forward.

Header box (decided 2026-10-19, revised 2026-11-07; new entries only, see
  below):
  The header line sits inside a fixed-width 80-dash rule, repeated above
  and below it:
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    YYYY-MM-DD Day | text... :tag: :tag:
    --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  Purely visual, like *** itself -- agenda reads only the header line
  between the rules, nothing about the rules or the box. Width is fixed
  at 80 characters regardless of the header line's own length; don't
  resize the rule per entry.
  Why boxed: LIFE.TXT gets read back regularly, not only grepped, and a
  boxed header is a much stronger visual anchor when scanning a long file
  than a bare date line. (Researched against OBTF/plain-text-journaling
  precedent; obtf.ru independently recommends the same boxed shape for
  files people read regularly, not just search.)
  Each header line carries its own date, summary, and tags -- never
  reused from the previous entry -- so that two same-day entries stay
  distinguishable both on screen and under grep.
  Older entries are NOT retrofitted to this shape -- pre-2026-10-19
  header lines with no box stay exactly as they are.

Spacing (revised 2026-11-07 -- compacted from an earlier three-blank-line
  version; superseded, don't reintroduce it):
  NO blank line between *** and the box's opening rule.
  NO blank line between the box's closing rule and the entry's body
    (if the entry has no body beyond the header, the next line is
    simply the blank line below).
  ONE blank line between the entry's last line (body, or the box's
    closing rule if there is no body) and the next ***.
  Rationale: *** is already an unambiguous visual marker on its own;
  a blank line on both sides of it was redundant. The one blank line
  that's kept is the one doing distinct work -- marking the seam
  between two entries -- which is the single gap most worth keeping
  when scanning a long file. Decided; don't reopen.

Semantic line breaks: start each sentence on its own line, breaking at
  sentence end (the period).
  Follows Kernighan, "UNIX for Beginners" (1974):
  "Start each sentence on a new line."
  (He also suggests breaking at commas/semicolons for long lines;
   skipped here -- one clean rule, one break per sentence.)
  Keeps edits, diffs, and greps landing per-sentence.
Header line (the single line inside the box) carries the date + tags and
  stays WHOLE:
  format:  YYYY-MM-DD Day | text... :tag: :tag:   [due:YYYY-MM-DD]
           [threshold:YYYY-MM-DD]   [completed:YYYY-MM-DD]
  Why whole: agenda is line-based -- :todo:/:waiting:/due:/threshold:/
  completed: must sit together on one line, or they parse as unrelated
  matches.
  So header = one line always, now framed by the box rather than left bare.
Continuation lines (after the box, until the next ***) are free prose:
  use semantic line breaks and blank-line paragraphs as needed. agenda never
  reads these, so structure them purely for future-you.
Tag placement in multi-line entries: put tags on the HEADER line (inside
  the box), so grep ':reference:' LIFE.TXT  returns the dated line, not a
  mid-entry fragment.
  (Same reason Project tags go on the header, not buried below.)
Skeleton (generalized form of everything above; the worked examples below
  are this same shape with real content):
  <previous entry's body text>

  ***
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  YYYY-MM-DD Day | header line :tag: :tag:
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  <entry body>

  ***
  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
  <next entry's header>
  ...

